WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.840
The Western is a classic film genre, so it's no&nbsp;
surprise that over the decades some have endured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.520
to not only be great in its category, but among&nbsp;
the best movies ever. From old-timey classics to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.720
violent Spaghetti Westerns, these are the only&nbsp;
near-perfect Westerns according to Metacritic.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.120
Spaghetti Westerns reached new heights&nbsp;
with "The Good, the Bad and the Ugly,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.040
Sergio Leone's epic 1966 story of three&nbsp;
gunfighters on the trail of some Confederate gold.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.400
Leone had pioneered Italy's run of stylish,&nbsp;
stark, yet grandiose Westerns with the great "A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.240
Fistful of Dollars," which set up Clint Eastwood&nbsp;
as the Man with No Name, a lone and unpredictable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.360
antihero who was good with a gun and always on the&nbsp;
move. In "The Good, the Bad and the Ugly," he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:49.120
back — and not even nicknaming him Blondie can&nbsp;
hide how dangerous he is. The tension runs high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.880
as Blondie forms a wary partnership with bandit&nbsp;
Tuco to go after the gold. They might betray each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.000
other ... or they might run afoul of the cruel,&nbsp;
capable Angel Eyes, who's also on the trail.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.200
The result is a movie as hauntingly beautiful&nbsp;
as it is violent. It serves up both a mythic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.080
American West and one that's stripped of all&nbsp;
the old-fashioned lore of good guys and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.600
white hats. It's a genre-defining film that&nbsp;
absolutely deserves its 90 on Metacritic.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.680
Released in 2007, "No Country for Old Men"&nbsp;
has a 91 on Metacritic, and it definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.000
deserves its place among the top-ranked&nbsp;
Westerns. The Coen brothers go especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.640
dark for this tale of stolen drug money,&nbsp;
violent times, and the forces of chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.680
and fate. The movie pulled in an incredible&nbsp;
Oscar haul, taking home statues for Best Picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.840
Best Director, Best Supporting&nbsp;
Actor, and Best Adapted Screenplay.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.200
The story is a film noir plot set in a Western&nbsp;
world. Llewelyn Moss is out hunting one day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.600
when he stumbles on the grisly aftermath of&nbsp;
a shootout — and a briefcase full of cash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.600
He takes it ... and that decision&nbsp;
changes everything. Enter the terrifying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.240
off-kilter Anton Chigurh, a hired killer&nbsp;
who's supposed to retrieve the money and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.200
who's so ruthless and violent in pursuit of it&nbsp;
that his own employers regret bringing him in.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.600
"Nah I don't think that's how I'd describe him."

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:06.800
"How would you describe him?"

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.280
"I guess I'd say he doesn't&nbsp;
have a sense of humor."

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.360
Trying to make sense of it all is the&nbsp;
weary Ed Tom Bell, a sheriff who's seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.240
just about all he can stand but who will&nbsp;
have to see this horror all the same.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.920
"No Country for Old Men" is bleak,&nbsp;
funny, and deeply memorable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.000
especially for its villain. With his&nbsp;
bowl haircut, air-pressure bolt gun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.040
and insistence on high-stakes coin&nbsp;
flips, Chigurh instantly became an icon.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.400
If you missed "The Rider," there's never been&nbsp;
a better time to check it out — director Chloé&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.760
Zhao’s Oscar win for "Nomadland" gives you&nbsp;
a great excuse for revisiting her backlist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.040
And you'll have the benefit of hitting on one of&nbsp;
Metacritic's top Westerns, with a Metascore of 92.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.520
"The Rider" is the story of Brady, whose life&nbsp;
on the pro rodeo circuit is cut short when an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.480
accident leaves him with a brain injury that&nbsp;
causes seizures. He's not supposed to ride again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.600
A lot of movies would follow an inspirational&nbsp;
sports movie template and end with Brady back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.800
at the height of his powers, but "The Rider" is&nbsp;
more restrained and realistic. Instead, we follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.320
Brady's struggle to adjust to falling back into&nbsp;
a life of limited options and even less glory.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.680
Film critic Godfrey Cheshire notes that&nbsp;
the documentary-like realism is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.920
film's strengths. What you're seeing, from&nbsp;
horse-training to trauma to friendship, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:30.000
often real. Zhao handles all of this delicately,&nbsp;
with both empathy and respect. It's a vision of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.720
the modern American West that's rooted in real&nbsp;
life and real people, and that makes it sing.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.240
Clocking in on Metacritic with a score of&nbsp;
93, "Rio Bravo" isn't just one of the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:46.400
Westerns — it's one of the all-time great team-up&nbsp;
movies. It was director Howard Hawks' cinematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.200
rebuttal to "High Noon," which he thought made&nbsp;
its sheriff too wishy-washy and ineffective.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.400
"Rio Bravo" has a laid-back, unhurried pace.&nbsp;
It has shootouts and action, but it's also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.200
classic hangout movie where a lot of the fun comes&nbsp;
from watching the low-key interaction between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.720
likable characters. One of the contrasts with&nbsp;
"High Noon" is that sense of friendliness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.000
Here, when trouble strikes and there's&nbsp;
going to be a raid on the local jail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.000
John Wayne's Sheriff John T. Chance rounds up a&nbsp;
team of motley allies. They might seem unqualified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.520
or all washed-up, but he sees something in them.&nbsp;
And with charming performances from Dean Martin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.840
Ricky Nelson, Angie Dickinson, and Walter&nbsp;
Brennan, we see something in them too — fun.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.600
John Ford's "Stagecoach," with a 93 on&nbsp;
Metacritic, has a simple and irresistible premise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.840
Take one stagecoach, fill it with a cast of&nbsp;
colorful characters, and put it in enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.840
danger that all the characters will have to&nbsp;
rise to the occasion in order to stay alive.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.680
The result is a terrific movie, one that's&nbsp;
both exciting and emotionally involving.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.800
It also provided John Wayne's breakout role&nbsp;
as the Ringo Kid, the heart-of-gold outlaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.920
whose gentle courtship of saloon girl Dallas is&nbsp;
one of the movie's highlights. And it cemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.400
John Ford as as a Western specialist, as&nbsp;
he'd go on to make other classics, making&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.880
]the film feels even more important. But it&nbsp;
doesn't need historical context to sell it. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.840
one's not a classic because of the role it played&nbsp;
in movie history. It's just that enjoyable.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.000
"McCabe &amp; Mrs. Miller" is Robert&nbsp;
Altman's version of the West:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.680
and with a Metacritic score&nbsp;
of 93, it's a very good one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.480
Muddy and mumbly and morally ambiguous, with&nbsp;
its blend of brutality and poetry and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:28.160
sense of the tumultuous life of a whole town,&nbsp;
it feels like an ancestor of TV's "Deadwood."

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.880
The film offers wonderful performances from&nbsp;
Warren Beatty and Julie Christie as the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.520
title characters. They start out as business&nbsp;
partners in McCabe's brothel and eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.480
drift into a kind of romance. But the love&nbsp;
story isn't necessarily a hopeful one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:48.000
and the boom in business doesn't keep them out&nbsp;
of trouble. None of this is destined to end well.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.960
That nearly dreamlike quality is important because&nbsp;
Altman was reinventing how movies worked. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.520
parts of his unconventional approach, like the&nbsp;
way the dialogue is deliberately hard to make out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.720
might frustrate viewers, the&nbsp;
complexity here is rewarding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.800
"McCabe and Mrs. Miller" will linger in your&nbsp;
mind long after the credits have rolled.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.360
With a Metascore of 94,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:12.560
we have 1950's "The Gunfighter," which depicts&nbsp;
an age-old problem: When you're the best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.400
you're also the one everyone wants to beat.&nbsp;
Sooner or later, there's nowhere to go but down.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.600
Gregory Peck plays Jimmy Ringo, famous as the&nbsp;
best gunfighter in the West. He's so famous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.400
in fact, that he always attracts trouble. There&nbsp;
are too many men looking to kill him and take up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:32.720
his mantle. He's constantly having to fight for&nbsp;
his life — and when his challengers are dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.920
their families sometimes show up seeking revenge.&nbsp;
All he wants, at this point, is to reconnect with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.120
his estranged wife and finally meet his son, but&nbsp;
his reputation threatens to make it impossible.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.840
The tension and tragedy of the movie work&nbsp;
together to give it an almost hypnotic pull.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:51.840
Its look at the hollowness of celebrity&nbsp;
and the sorrow of a life spent on killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.320
once put the movie ahead of its time. Now&nbsp;
that approach makes the movie immortal.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.880
"The Searchers" may not sit quite&nbsp;
at the top of Metacritic's list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:04.720
but it still has a solid claim to&nbsp;
being the best Western ever made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.040
In 2008, the American Film Institute&nbsp;
honored it with that distinction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:15.040
and Sight &amp; Sound went even further by declaring&nbsp;
it the seventh best film of all time, period.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:20.320
It's easy to see why the movie attracts so many&nbsp;
admirers. The compelling story that follows John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.840
Wayne's Ethan Edwards going after his kidnapped&nbsp;
niece, takes a heroic premise and twists it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:31.200
The result exposes all the brutality and racism&nbsp;
that can lie beneath the legends of the Old West.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:36.720
Because the unsettling truth is that Ethan isn't&nbsp;
trying to save Debbie ... he only wants to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:42.800
her so she won't have to live with having been,&nbsp;
quote, "despoiled" by her Comanche abductors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.680
That deep-seated anger and prejudice make&nbsp;
for one of Wayne's most complex performances.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.400
"From now on you stay out of this -&nbsp;
all of ya! I don't want ya with me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:54.960
Don't need ya for what I gotta do."

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.240
Director John Ford portrays all this viscerally,&nbsp;
and accompanies it with some extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.320
landscapes, giving "The Searchers" a&nbsp;
desolate but beautiful sense of place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.840
The combination makes for an&nbsp;
incredibly influential movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:12.320
Harsh, striking, and powerful, this isn't&nbsp;
just a movie; It's a cultural landmark.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.760
Even if you haven't seen "The Man Who Shot Liberty&nbsp;
Valance," you might know its most famous quote:

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.680
"This is the West sir. When the legend&nbsp;
becomes fact, print the legend."

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:28.240
The quote fits the movie, which is a classic&nbsp;
with a 94 on Metacritic. "The Man Who Shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:34.080
Liberty Valance" is all about how tall tales and&nbsp;
legends helped shape the Old West. It zooms in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.040
on one story in particular — the intersection&nbsp;
of the fates of future senator Ransom Stoddard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.360
practical rancher Tom Doniphon,&nbsp;
and wicked outlaw Liberty Valance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:47.600
Valance terrorizes the town of Shinbone,&nbsp;
while Stoddard wants to bring it closer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.400
civilization and order. Tom stands somewhere&nbsp;
in between the two — his intentions are good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:56.320
but he lives by a violent code that&nbsp;
Stoddard can't approve of. Something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:01.120
has to give ... and when it does, it will make&nbsp;
history. But will the history be the truth?

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.600
John Ford directed a number&nbsp;
of celebrated Westerns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.000
but "The Man Who Shot Liberty Valance"&nbsp;
as Roger Ebert points out is, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.280
"the most pensive and thoughtful." If you want&nbsp;
to see how lawless territories became lawful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:17.120
and what was won and lost in the&nbsp;
process, this is the film for you.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.360
"The Wild Bunch" earned its Metascore of 97 by&nbsp;
drastically shaking up the Western template. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:29.520
1969 film pumped the genre full of controversial&nbsp;
and unprecedented levels of cynicism and violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:35.040
giving us a Vietnam War-era look at all the&nbsp;
supposed cowboy heroes of times gone by.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.360
The men in "The Wild Bunch" mostly live&nbsp;
sordid lives. They're vicious outlaws with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.120
very little honor. They don't hesitate&nbsp;
to kill, and they're quick to use human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.280
shields when they think it will help. Their&nbsp;
whole world is one of cruelty and violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:52.240
but just before this unrelenting parade of&nbsp;
misery and cutthroat behavior becomes too much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:57.280
director Sam Peckinpah tosses in an actual&nbsp;
moral dilemma. One member of the gang is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.440
kidnapped and tortured. Can the others bring&nbsp;
themselves to risk their lives for him?

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.880
While the movie certainly attracted&nbsp;
plenty of criticism for its viciousness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:09.920
time has definitely been on its side. In a&nbsp;
retrospective review, Rolling Stone said -

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:15.440
"The hard action, bracing wit, and mournful grace&nbsp;
of Peckinpah's cowboy classic shames every new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:20.240
movie around. It's a towering achievement that&nbsp;
grows more riveting and resonant with the years."

00:10:21.436 --> 00:10:26.560
The top-rated Western on Metacritic, with a score&nbsp;
of 98, "The Treasure of the Sierra Madre" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:32.240
famous for its grim, tragic portrayals of greed&nbsp;
in action. Humphrey Bogart plays Fred C. Dobbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:37.280
a down-on-his-luck man whose health and sanity&nbsp;
unravel over the course of the film. That's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:42.160
can happen when you go prospecting - even when&nbsp;
you find gold, you tend to lose everything else.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.840
"Badges? We ain't got no badges.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:50.880
We don't need no badges. I don't have&nbsp;
to show you any stinking badges!"

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.120
Dobbs and his friends go out into the&nbsp;
Sierra Madre mountains to dig for gold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:59.840
The surroundings are inhospitable, the work&nbsp;
is exhausting, and the men are increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:05.680
paranoid. The tension is almost unbearable, and&nbsp;
Bogart vividly evokes Dobbs' fraying mental state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:10.000
It all leads up to a tragic, ironic twist&nbsp;
that even "The Twilight Zone" would envy.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.160
The film still stands as one of the few&nbsp;
to have a perfect Rotten Tomatoes score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.320
and to sum it all up, Slant says -

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:20.560
"this deservingly canonized behemoth&nbsp;
is one of the relatively few films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:24.480
that transcends the medium to become&nbsp;
a mandatory viewing experience for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:27.840
anyone that identifies themselves&nbsp;
as a human being, period."

